---
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ButtonDemo from "../../components/ButtonDemo";
import source from "../../../registry/ui/button.tsx?raw";

const urlInstall = "npx shadcn@latest add https://example.com/r/button.json";
const namespaceInstall = `npx shadcn@latest registry add @buril=https://example.com/r/{name}.json
npx shadcn@latest add @buril/button`;
const themeInstall = "npx shadcn@latest add @buril/theme";

// Every token color, comments included, meets 4.5:1 on this theme's background (the worst is
// about 6.1:1). The docs tests measure it. github-dark fails: its comments are 3.05:1.
const codeTheme = "github-dark-default";

const statusExample = `<Button type="submit" loading={saving}>
  Save changes
</Button>
<p role="status">{saving ? "Saving changes…" : message}</p>`;
---

<BaseLayout title="Button | Buril UI">
  <h1 class="font-display text-3xl">Button</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    A native button with variants, sizes and a loading state. Styled anchors use the same classes
    through <code>buttonVariants</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Demo</h2>
  <div class="mt-4 border border-border p-6">
    <ButtonDemo client:visible />
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Loading</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    <code>loading</code> shows a spinner and blocks every activation path, including implicit form
    submission. The button keeps its accessible name and stays focusable, so keyboard and screen
    reader users keep their place. It sets <code>aria-busy</code> and <code>aria-disabled</code>,
    but screen readers generally do not announce <code>aria-busy</code> on a button.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    Announce progress yourself with a status region next to the button. Render it before the message
    changes, and keep it outside the button so the button's name stays stable:
  </p>
  <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
    <Code code={statusExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Install</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">Install the component from its URL:</p>
  <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
    <Code code={urlInstall} lang="bash" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>
  <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">Or add the Buril namespace once and install by name:</p>
  <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
    <Code code={namespaceInstall} lang="bash" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>
  <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    The button uses the standard shadcn tokens and the <code>cn()</code> helper you already have, so
    installing it does not change your theme. It creates
    <code>components/ui/button.tsx</code> and adds <code>class-variance-authority</code>. If your
    <code>lib/utils.ts</code> differs from the current shadcn version, the CLI asks before replacing
    it; answering no is safe, because the button only needs <code>cn()</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Optional theme</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    The Buril palette (ink primary, red lacquer for focus, decoration and destructive actions) is a
    separate install:
  </p>
  <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
    <Code code={themeInstall} lang="bash" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>
  <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">It edits your global CSS file:</p>
  <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
    <li>
      It replaces your shadcn color tokens in <code>:root</code> and <code>.dark</code>. Other
      variables, such as <code>--radius</code>, stay as they are.
    </li>
    <li>
      It adds two decorative tokens, <code>--decor</code> and <code>--grid</code>, and font tokens.
    </li>
    <li>
      It follows the system without JavaScript: under <code>prefers-color-scheme: dark</code>, the
      dark palette applies unless <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> has the <code>light</code> class.
    </li>
    <li>
      It redefines the <code>dark:</code> variant the same way, so <code>dark:</code> utilities also
      follow the system. A theme toggle must set <code>class="light"</code> on
      <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> to force light mode; removing <code>dark</code> is not enough. With
      next-themes, <code>attribute="class"</code> already does this.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Source</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    Read from <code>registry/ui/button.tsx</code> at build time.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
    <Code code={source} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
